<Project Sdk="AsmFoundation.Sdk">

  <!--
    HttpAsm.Core: the protocol core (docs/DESIGN.md, "Architecture"). It does no I/O, makes no
    system call and allocates nothing: bytes go in, and bytes and events come out, in memory the
    caller provides. Everything that touches the OS lives in src/HttpAsm.

    Why these settings:
      TargetFramework freestanding  the core links no C library. The build enforces the "no OS"
                                    rule: a freestanding project cannot reference libc code
                                    (NuGet's NU1201), and an OS call would fail the final link
                                    of libhttpasm.so, which runs with -z defs.
      OutputType Objects            libhttpasm.so links every object of the core. A static
                                    library would only lend the archive members that libhttpasm.so's
                                    own objects call, and the core's exported functions
                                    (httpasm_token_length, ...) are called by nobody inside it.

    Layout (shared source, separate code; docs/DESIGN.md, "Sharing between the versions"):
      include/httpasm_core.inc      constants and the core's internal symbols
      include/semantics/*.inc       RFC 9110 rules as macros, written once and assembled into
                                    each HTTP version's own code
      semantics/*.asm               tables, and the rules as callable functions of the C ABI
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Objects</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>freestanding</TargetFramework>
    <AssemblyName>httpasm_core</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
